ion  and uh  and so at one point when i first got to  bls i started going through the  kind of the wickets to the process to  get it approved for for production  and you can see this um  this graphic right here my co-author  brandon cop  went back through our approval system  and looked at  the approval history for r starting in  2015 which is kind of when i started  this  this whole uh this whole process  now the blue bars are  approvals for research purposes the  green bars were approvals for production  so you can see uh right from the  beginning here in 2015 or so it was  approved for production  of uh and then all of a sudden somebody  said in our it oh wait that's that's not  good we have to you know take that back  and you can only use it for research  then again  production  no and then just research purposes  so what's what i  what's just kind of interesting to me  here is it's sort of an indication of  this kind of circular  road  for getting r approved for production  that i've gone through  uh over the years  and this was a graphic that i showed in  my talk last year so it hasn't been  updated uh  yet  why should we use our uh at the bureau  of labor statistics or in the federal  government  uh well first of all you know the cost  the uh  then there's the fact that you know when  somebody say a researcher develops a new  methodology  oftentimes they  write an r package or something or  functions to to implement that implement  that methodology  and  so  that is available right away for  researchers let's say a bls to use  uh in in um  producing  what we do at bls  uh which is and then often the that type  of functionality is  sometimes  not ever available in commercial  software  but in any case it takes a long time for  it to be implemented and say something  like sas  we've also faced with hiring uh people  right out of school  and usually in their  universities they don't know they don't  learn sas but they might use oftentimes  they learn r or say py